handbook.subjects.body:
A dependency keeps its own government, army and flag and answers to another country. There are several kinds, and each says what it owes and what it gets:

- protectorate, puppet, satellite and client state - from a foreign policy made elsewhere to a government installed and kept there;
- dominion - self-governing, and turning up when the senior partner is attacked;
- associated state - its own government, and its defence and much of its money from its patron, like the Compact states of the Pacific or the breakaways Russia and Turkey keep;
- leased territory - rented land run as part of the state that rents it, like Baikonur.

A dependency pays tribute and manpower in some arrangements, joins its overlord's wars in others, and has a liberty desire that grows when it grows, when its overlord weakens or loses a war. Past a point it leaves. An overlord can absorb a small and content dependency after enough years.
